    {
      "$comment": "The one pair a veil can be measured in: not \"is the veil readable\" — it carries no text — but \"can you see where the dialog ends\". The panel and the dimmed page behind it are both dark in the dark theme, and the edge is the only thing that separates them. Measured against the veil ALONE, with its alpha ignored: what is really behind the border is the veil composited over whatever the page had there, and the darkest that can be is the veil at full strength. So the number is the worst case, which is the one worth having.",
      "name": "UI: dialog panel edge against the dimmed page",
      "fg": "pct.dialog.panel-border",
      "bg": "pct.dialog.backdrop-bg",
      "level": "UI",
      "severity": "error"
    },
    {
      "name": "UI: dialog close button",
      "fg": "pct.dialog.close-fg",
      "bg": "pct.dialog.panel-bg",
      "level": "UI",
      "severity": "error"
    },
    {
      "name": "UI: dialog close button (hover)",
      "fg": "pct.dialog.close-fg-hover",
      "bg": "pct.dialog.close-bg-hover",
      "level": "UI",
      "severity": "error"
    },
    {
      "name": "popover — text on the panel",
      "fg": "pct.popover.panel-fg",
      "bg": "pct.popover.panel-bg",
      "level": "AA",
      "severity": "error"
    },
    {
      "name": "popover — heading",
      "fg": "pct.popover.heading-fg",
      "bg": "pct.popover.panel-bg",
      "level": "AA",
      "severity": "error"
    },
    {
      "$comment": "A menu draws the page's own surface, so its edge is measured against the page — the popover's reading, one component over. The row is measured against ITS OWN background rather than against the panel's, and the two happen to be the same colour today: the pair has to name what the row really paints, or the day somebody tints the rows is the day this entry stops describing anything. The text stands twice over, because a menu spends half its life with one row painted.",
      "name": "menu — a command on its own row",
      "fg": "pct.menu.item-fg",
      "bg": "pct.menu.item-bg",
      "level": "AA",
      "severity": "error"
    },
    {
      "$comment": "The panel paints a text colour of its own, and it is what anything a consumer puts between the rows inherits — a heading, a line of explanation. It is measured even though no part of the component draws text directly on it.",
      "name": "menu — the panel's own text",
      "fg": "pct.menu.panel-fg",
      "bg": "pct.menu.panel-bg",
      "level": "AA",
      "severity": "error"
    },
    {
      "name": "menu — a command on the row the user is on",
      "fg": "pct.menu.item-fg",
      "bg": "pct.menu.item-bg-active",
      "level": "AA",
      "severity": "error"
    },
    {
      "$comment": "A disabled command, on the same footing as every other disabled state here: a warning and not an error, because the greying IS the message. This pair happens to clear AA against the panel, and pinning it as an error would forbid the one change that would make a disabled row read as one (req-token-contrast).",
      "name": "menu/disabled — a command that cannot be chosen",
      "fg": "pct.menu.item-fg-disabled",
      "bg": "pct.menu.item-bg",
      "level": "AA",
      "severity": "warn"
    },
    {
      "name": "UI: menu panel edge against the page",
      "fg": "pct.menu.panel-border",
      "bg": "pct.surface",
      "level": "UI",
      "severity": "error"
    },
    {
      "$comment": "A popover has no veil under it, so its edge is measured against the PAGE — the surface it really stands on. The dialog's own edge is measured against the veil instead, and the two entries together are the whole difference between the modal and the non-modal panel.",
      "name": "UI: popover panel edge against the page",
      "fg": "pct.popover.panel-border",
      "bg": "pct.surface",
      "level": "UI",
      "severity": "error"
    },
    {
      "$comment": "The whole of a tooltip is one pair: a panel with a sentence on it and nothing else. It stands here twice over — once as text on a surface, and once as the only measurement the inverted pair ever gets, since `pct.surface-inverse` is painted by no other component yet.",
